WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.301 --> 00:00:02.535
-Our next guest is
an Emmy-nominated actor

00:00:02.535 --> 00:00:04.237
you know from
the show "Normal People"

00:00:04.237 --> 00:00:05.405
and the film
"The Lost Daughter."

00:00:05.405 --> 00:00:07.040
He stars in "Aftersun,"

00:00:07.040 --> 00:00:09.309
which is in select theaters
October 21st.

00:00:09.309 --> 00:00:10.577
Let's take a look.

00:00:10.577 --> 00:00:11.911
-Good.

00:00:11.911 --> 00:00:13.346
[ Billiard balls clacking ]
Well done.

00:00:13.346 --> 00:00:17.316 line:0%
♪♪

00:00:17.316 --> 00:00:19.118
-So, what's your sister's name?

00:00:19.118 --> 00:00:20.754
-Sophie.
-Oh, nice.

00:00:22.054 --> 00:00:23.789
-I'm her dad, though, actually.

00:00:23.789 --> 00:00:25.124
-Oh. Sorry, mate.
I just thought --

00:00:25.124 --> 00:00:26.325
-That's okay.

00:00:26.325 --> 00:00:30.029 line:0%
♪♪

00:00:30.029 --> 00:00:31.864
It's a nice hotel, eh?
-Oh, it's alright.

00:00:31.864 --> 00:00:34.434
-Please welcome to the show,
Paul Mescal, everybody!

00:00:34.434 --> 00:00:37.270
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:37.270 --> 00:00:43.943 line:0%
♪♪

00:00:43.943 --> 00:00:50.650 line:0%
♪♪

00:00:50.650 --> 00:00:52.418
I'm so happy to have you here.

00:00:52.418 --> 00:00:55.287
-Thanks very much.
-Congratulations on this film.

00:00:55.287 --> 00:00:58.791
It's a very beautiful film about
a young father and his daughter.

00:00:58.791 --> 00:01:01.794
And a lot of it is unsaid.

00:01:01.794 --> 00:01:04.330
I would say that is not
a dialogue-heavy film.

00:01:04.330 --> 00:01:08.101
When you were reading through
the script the first time,

00:01:08.101 --> 00:01:11.671
did you appreciate exactly
how special it was going to be?

00:01:11.671 --> 00:01:14.240
-Yeah, it's one of those scripts
that you read and you're like,

00:01:14.240 --> 00:01:15.574
"How do I get to the top

00:01:15.574 --> 00:01:17.943
of the pile of actors
that are going to want it?"

00:01:17.943 --> 00:01:21.714
So, yeah, I was very aware
of the kind of -- the fact

00:01:21.714 --> 00:01:24.516
that it was kind of nonverbal
in how it presents itself.

00:01:24.516 --> 00:01:27.820
And, yeah, I just very quickly
auditioned for Charlie

00:01:27.820 --> 00:01:29.822
and here I am talking about it.

00:01:29.822 --> 00:01:31.091
-You play a young father
in the film.

00:01:31.091 --> 00:01:33.225
You're not a young father
in real life.

00:01:33.225 --> 00:01:34.393
And you actually took

00:01:34.393 --> 00:01:37.363
a rehearsal vacation
with your costar.

00:01:37.363 --> 00:01:40.266
-Yeah, Yeah. We landed in Turkey
kind of two weeks

00:01:40.266 --> 00:01:41.634
before the shoot,

00:01:41.634 --> 00:01:45.071
and I asked Frankie's mom
and dad if I could,

00:01:45.071 --> 00:01:48.507
like, be her pretend dad
for two weeks.

00:01:48.507 --> 00:01:50.376
And so, yeah, we hung out.

00:01:50.376 --> 00:01:51.911
-We were the kind of parents

00:01:51.911 --> 00:01:53.913
who were like, "Yep."
-They were like, "Please."

00:01:53.913 --> 00:01:55.581
No, no, no. They were great.

00:01:55.581 --> 00:01:58.083
Like, we swam in the sea,

00:01:58.083 --> 00:02:01.020
ate pancakes, swam in the pool,
lots of swimming.

00:02:01.020 --> 00:02:03.823
And we taught her
how to play pool.

00:02:03.823 --> 00:02:06.759
-She's fantastic.
It's also her first film.

00:02:06.759 --> 00:02:09.996
Is it -- How is it to have
a scene partner,

00:02:09.996 --> 00:02:12.798
especially one in a -- in a film

00:02:12.798 --> 00:02:15.401
where the connection
is so intense, who is...

00:02:15.401 --> 00:02:17.670
I mean, I would just assume
a natural at it?

00:02:17.670 --> 00:02:18.904
-Total natural.

00:02:18.904 --> 00:02:20.673
It's, like, frightening.
You're like, "Whoa.

00:02:20.673 --> 00:02:22.041
If you knew how good

00:02:22.041 --> 00:02:23.743
you are right now,
it would be, like, shocking."

00:02:23.743 --> 00:02:27.013
But, yeah, I feel like
with young actors

00:02:27.013 --> 00:02:30.483
and people starting out,
you're kind of harnessing

00:02:30.483 --> 00:02:31.917
or they're holding on
to this thing.

00:02:31.917 --> 00:02:34.186
Whereas with older actors
you can kind of...

00:02:34.186 --> 00:02:36.088
talk acting out of existence,

00:02:36.088 --> 00:02:37.923
here she's just so pure

00:02:37.923 --> 00:02:39.224
and full of life.

00:02:39.224 --> 00:02:41.361
And it's -- Yeah, it was...
-Is it contagious?

00:02:41.361 --> 00:02:42.996
Did you find yourself going back

00:02:42.996 --> 00:02:46.933
to sort of the roots of, like,
what's natural about acting?

00:02:46.933 --> 00:02:48.434
-Yeah, well,
she just surprises you.

00:02:48.434 --> 00:02:51.604
She would do something, like,
in a scene where you're, like,

00:02:51.604 --> 00:02:54.040
"Whoa, I wish
I thought of that."

00:02:54.040 --> 00:02:57.543
She's just in it all the time,
and I just can't wait for people

00:02:57.543 --> 00:02:59.578
to see her performance
in the film.

00:02:59.578 --> 00:03:00.980
-This is an exciting time.

00:03:00.980 --> 00:03:02.448
You're in a couple of movies
right now.

00:03:02.448 --> 00:03:04.984
"God's Creatures," as well.

00:03:04.984 --> 00:03:08.087
I have to ask a question,
because you are shucking oysters

00:03:08.087 --> 00:03:11.457
a great deal in this film
very believably so.

00:03:11.457 --> 00:03:13.860
Was that a skill you had
before you took this part?

00:03:13.860 --> 00:03:17.797
-It was -- No, that was not
a skill that I had.

00:03:17.797 --> 00:03:19.299
-That's a dangerous one to learn

00:03:19.299 --> 00:03:22.068
because I feel like there's no
shortcuts with shucking oysters.

00:03:22.068 --> 00:03:25.204
-No, there's -- there's
no shortcuts. Yeah.

00:03:25.204 --> 00:03:26.973
Fodhla, our wonderful producer,

00:03:26.973 --> 00:03:29.508
her brother is an oyster farmer.

00:03:29.508 --> 00:03:31.443
So I was like,
"I'm going to be a proper actor

00:03:31.443 --> 00:03:33.146
and learn how to do this."

00:03:33.146 --> 00:03:36.216
And went out on the water
with John Michael

00:03:36.216 --> 00:03:37.950
and he taught me how to do it,
yeah.

00:03:37.950 --> 00:03:41.053
-Are you pretty good now?
-Eh, middling, yeah.

00:03:41.053 --> 00:03:42.655
Yeah.
-Thank you for your honesty.

00:03:42.655 --> 00:03:44.424
[ Laughter ]

00:03:44.424 --> 00:03:48.694
I want to talk about --
you had, in early life,

00:03:48.694 --> 00:03:50.429
a parallel track
of both acting --

00:03:50.429 --> 00:03:53.032
You were in acting school,
but you were also playing...

00:03:53.032 --> 00:03:54.733
I guess this isn't
technically rugby.

00:03:54.733 --> 00:03:55.868
This is Gaelic?

00:03:55.868 --> 00:03:57.603
-Gaelic football, yeah.
-Gaelic football?

00:03:57.603 --> 00:04:01.140
How would you describe, for
those of us who are maybe not up

00:04:01.140 --> 00:04:03.910
on the latest?
-Gaelic football?

00:04:03.910 --> 00:04:05.144
-Yeah, Gaelic football.

00:04:05.144 --> 00:04:06.545
-Chaos.
-Chaos.

00:04:06.545 --> 00:04:08.380
-Pure chaos.
It's basically like a mix

00:04:08.380 --> 00:04:11.917
between rugby and soccer,

00:04:11.917 --> 00:04:14.787
but is just...brutal.

00:04:14.787 --> 00:04:16.289
-Yeah.
-Well, rugby is pretty brutal,

00:04:16.289 --> 00:04:19.091
but, like, you represent
like your parish

00:04:19.091 --> 00:04:20.093
or where you come from.

00:04:20.093 --> 00:04:22.128
It's, like, nonprofessional.

00:04:22.128 --> 00:04:26.099
So it's like very much like...
very spirited, I would say.

00:04:26.099 --> 00:04:28.333
-So it's not that
there's a different rule.

00:04:28.333 --> 00:04:32.171
It's just like people
are maybe more prone to fight?

00:04:32.171 --> 00:04:34.306
-Yeah, yeah.

00:04:34.306 --> 00:04:36.909
It depends on which town
you're playing against.

00:04:36.909 --> 00:04:38.410
-Gotcha. Did you --

00:04:38.410 --> 00:04:40.413
Did you play for a good town?
Were you guys good at it?

00:04:40.413 --> 00:04:42.548
-Yeah. I liked my town.
[ Laughter ]

00:04:42.548 --> 00:04:44.083
-I meant good at the sport.

00:04:44.083 --> 00:04:47.086
Were you a nice place to live?
[ Laughter ]

00:04:47.086 --> 00:04:51.524
-Yeah. Yeah, I played
for Maynooth and...

00:04:51.524 --> 00:04:53.092
and Kildare, my county.

00:04:53.092 --> 00:04:54.460
This is like a geography lesson.

00:04:54.460 --> 00:04:56.095
-Gotcha.
-For anybody who doesn't know,

00:04:56.095 --> 00:04:57.830
that's Kildare.
-Seems like mouth guard

00:04:57.830 --> 00:04:58.964
is a pretty important
part of that I'm assuming.

00:04:58.964 --> 00:05:01.067
-Yeah, very necessary.

00:05:01.067 --> 00:05:03.636
-And was it --
were you prone to injury,

00:05:03.636 --> 00:05:05.904
or do the people who
play Gaelic football get...?

00:05:05.904 --> 00:05:08.874
-Yeah, I broke my nose
and my jaw.

00:05:08.874 --> 00:05:11.076
I broke my jaw two days

00:05:11.076 --> 00:05:13.345
before starting
my final year in drama school.

00:05:13.345 --> 00:05:16.215
So I was like, "I'm going
to probably give it a break

00:05:16.215 --> 00:05:17.649
for the last year."

00:05:17.649 --> 00:05:20.386
I found out that they don't
really go hand-in-hand.

00:05:20.386 --> 00:05:22.021
You can't really act
with a broken jaw.

00:05:22.021 --> 00:05:25.658
-When you showed up to
acting school with a broken jaw,

00:05:25.658 --> 00:05:27.693
were you honest that this
happened during Gaelic football?

00:05:27.693 --> 00:05:29.629
-Because they say you're not
allowed to play any sport

00:05:29.629 --> 00:05:31.630
because it's like bad
for your, like, body and stuff,

00:05:31.630 --> 00:05:33.199
and I was like,
"Yeah, it'll be fine."

00:05:33.199 --> 00:05:37.302
Go in, and I -- face-to-face
with my, like, acting teachers

00:05:37.302 --> 00:05:38.371
and they're like,
"What's wrong?"

00:05:38.371 --> 00:05:39.638
And I was like,
"Well, this is so weird.

00:05:39.638 --> 00:05:41.774
I got mugged at work.

00:05:41.774 --> 00:05:44.176
[ Laughter ]
Yeah. A nightmare."

00:05:44.176 --> 00:05:46.078
And so I spent the four weeks
in rehearsals

00:05:46.078 --> 00:05:47.947
just kind of like rehearsing
like this.

00:05:47.947 --> 00:05:49.214
Yeah, yeah.

00:05:49.214 --> 00:05:51.451
-Was Gaelic football sad
to see you go?

00:05:51.451 --> 00:05:52.718
Did you have a skill at it?

00:05:52.718 --> 00:05:54.420
Were they like,
"we lost one of our good ones

00:05:54.420 --> 00:05:57.023
to the acting craft"?
[ Laughter ]

00:05:57.023 --> 00:05:59.058
-I hope that's what they say.
Yeah.

00:05:59.058 --> 00:06:01.126
I hope that's what they say.
-You know,

00:06:01.126 --> 00:06:03.195
for matters of this,
let's just all agree

00:06:03.195 --> 00:06:05.097
that they're still talking
about you in the pubs.

00:06:05.097 --> 00:06:06.499
The one that got away.
-Exactly. Exactly.

00:06:06.499 --> 00:06:08.801
Yeah. Yeah.

00:06:08.801 --> 00:06:10.202
-We were talking backstage,
you know,

00:06:10.202 --> 00:06:12.738
because you -- "Normal People,"
which is so wonderful --

00:06:12.738 --> 00:06:13.772
and you actually haven't done

00:06:13.772 --> 00:06:15.474
any one of these
talk shows in person.

00:06:15.474 --> 00:06:16.642
You did a bunch of them on Zoom
during the...

00:06:16.642 --> 00:06:18.277
-Yeah.
-Was it --

00:06:18.277 --> 00:06:20.345
'Cause it was one of
our first pandemic shows.

00:06:20.345 --> 00:06:21.914
My wife and I watched it.

00:06:21.914 --> 00:06:23.783
Was it cool
to make a show like that,

00:06:23.783 --> 00:06:25.417
that obviously worst
possible reason

00:06:25.417 --> 00:06:26.619
for people to connect with it,

00:06:26.619 --> 00:06:28.087
but everybody was looking
for shows,

00:06:28.087 --> 00:06:29.789
and I feel like that was one
a lot of people found.

00:06:29.789 --> 00:06:32.324
-Totally and, like,
these things are wonderful,

00:06:32.324 --> 00:06:34.560
but they're kind of stressful
a little bit.

00:06:34.560 --> 00:06:36.362
So getting to do it
a kind of a year

00:06:36.362 --> 00:06:38.464
further into it is fantastic.

00:06:38.464 --> 00:06:39.898
And for it to be
"Normal People"

00:06:39.898 --> 00:06:41.534
the thing that people
still talk about

00:06:41.534 --> 00:06:43.736
that I'm really, really
proud of is...

00:06:43.736 --> 00:06:45.337
I'm very grateful for that.

00:06:45.337 --> 00:06:47.106
-Yeah, well,
it was a really special show

00:06:47.106 --> 00:06:48.574
and it's so great
to meet you in person.

00:06:48.574 --> 00:06:49.708
Thank you so much
for being here, Paul.

00:06:49.708 --> 00:06:50.976
-Thanks for having me.
Thank you so much.

00:06:50.976 --> 00:06:52.611
-Paul Mescal, everybody.

00:06:52.611 --> 00:06:55.180
"Aftersun" is in select theaters
is October 21st,

00:06:55.180 --> 00:06:56.949
and "God's Creatures"
is in theaters Friday.

00:06:56.949 --> 00:07:00.019
We'll be right back to music
from 5 Seconds of Summer.

